{"reference":"Hebrews 2","book":"Hebrews","chapter":2,"status":"pending","sections":[{"kind":"surrounding_passages","title":"Connection with the preceding passage","blocks":[{"id":"block_1","text":"Hebrews 2 begins as the conclusion of a sequence of scriptural quotations in Hebrews 1 that distinguishes the Son from angels. That preceding comparison leads directly into the warning to pay close attention to what has been heard; the chapter’s references to angels therefore continue an argument already underway.","verse_refs":["Hebrews 1:5-14"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_hebrews_002_20db9253d69e3fdee5bb4c4c"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"fact"},{"id":"block_2","text":"Papyrus 46 is an early, substantial manuscript witness to Hebrews, although parts of it are missing and its date can only be estimated from its handwriting. This is a reminder that the text of Hebrews is known through ancient manuscript transmission rather than a single surviving original copy.","provenance_refs":["reader_path_hebrews_002_b92cc1dc901386c1dee02d80"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"interpretive_questions","title":"Questions raised by the angel comparison","blocks":[{"id":"block_3","text":"The comparison with angels in verses 1–9 is meant to exalt the Son, not to authorize contempt for Jewish traditions about angels or speculation about their rank. Verse 5 also uses a term that can refer either to the inhabited world or to the coming order, so one English rendering of “world to come” does not by itself settle its full scope.","verse_refs":["Hebrews 2:1-9","Hebrews 2:5"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_hebrews_002_c4e1e56db1f04e75e6022097"],"confidence":"low","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"cultural_context","title":"Shared humanity and help","blocks":[{"id":"block_4","text":"The latter half of the chapter emphasizes Jesus’ solidarity with those he helps: he shares flesh and blood, suffers testing, calls the sanctified his brothers and sisters, and is described as a merciful and faithful high priest. These claims explain why the chapter presents him as able to help those who are tested.","verse_refs":["Hebrews 2:10-18"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_hebrews_002_6246a58b58367487420c938f"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"fact"},{"id":"block_5","text":"The claim that Jesus shared human vulnerability and suffered testing grounds the chapter’s picture of compassionate help. It should not be read as glorifying trauma or abuse, or as discouraging people from seeking safety.","verse_refs":["Hebrews 2:10-18"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_hebrews_002_c2afa600b8e12fde687136b0"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]}],"generated_metadata":null}